#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124171 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1f2ac99b-55b2-5742-81ab-d2961db1250f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Functions at Points of Continuity"}] :question/id #uuid "a5753b43-b288-5cca-a26b-b060917182ab" :question/math-academy-id "e-3330" :question/problem "Find $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,2)}} \\dfrac{xy^3}{x+y}$." :question/worked-solution "The function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)= \\dfrac{xy^3}{x+y}\n$$\n\nis continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(-1,2)$ does not cause the denominator to become zero, so we can calculate the limit by substituting $(-1,2)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → (-1, 2))\\frac{xy^{3}}{x + y} &= ((-1) \\cdot 2^{3})/((-1) + 2) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-8}{1} \\\\\n&=-8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124172 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5666035-199d-56aa-a6f4-f65a3f847b42" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Rational Functions by Factoring"}] :question/id #uuid "301263ef-3b68-54ac-87aa-5964484f5a14" :question/math-academy-id "e-3331" :question/problem "Find $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}} \\dfrac{x^2y+xy^2}{x^2-y^2}$." :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 0 0\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}} \\dfrac{x^2y+xy^2}{x^2-y^2} = \\dfrac{(-1)^2(1) + (-1)(1)^2}{(-1)^2 - (1)^2} = \\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → (-1, 1))\\frac{x^{2}y + xy^{2}}{x^{2} - y^{2}} &= lim_(x → (-1, 1))(xy(x + y))/((x + y)(x - y)) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → (-1, 1))(xy)/((x - y))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → (-1, 1))(xy)/((x - y)) &= ((-1) \\cdot 1)/(-1 - 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-1}{-2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124176 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29610715-333d-5e0c-9328-eec1d45f56ed" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits Along Different Paths for Multivariable Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "56539c63-dd81-57c9-b7d2-18c935953203" :question/math-academy-id "e-3366" :question/problem "Find $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}} \\dfrac{x(x + 2y)}{y^2}$." :question/worked-solution "Direct substitution gives an indeterminate form, but the function cannot be simplified by canceling a common factor in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}} \\dfrac{x(x + 2y)}{y^2} = \\dfrac{0 \\cdot (0 + 2 \\cdot 0)}{0^2} = \\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x$, we have\n$lim_(x → (0, 0)y = x)(x(x + 2y))/(y^{2})|= lim_(x → 0)(x(x + 2x))/(x^{2}); = lim_(x → 0)\\frac{3x^{2}}{x^{2}}; = 3$.\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=-x$, we have\n$lim_(x → (0, 0)y =-x)(x(x + 2y))/(y^{2})|= lim_(x → 0)(x(x + 2(-x)))/((-x)^{2}); = lim_(x → 0)\\frac{-x^{2}}{x^{2}}; =-1$.\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on these two paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}} \\dfrac{x(x + 2y)}{y^2} = \\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124285 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d53554f-6d77-56f9-8950-d8e31a7f4fdb" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Functions Using Conjugate Multiplication"}] :question/id #uuid "ae167d95-9cfd-5f31-91b1-484becd62677" :question/math-academy-id "e-6239" :question/problem "Find $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,1)}} \\dfrac{x^2-4y}{x-2\\sqrt{y}}$." :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac00\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,1)}} \\dfrac{2^2-4 \\cdot 1}{2-2\\sqrt{1}} = \\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → (2, 1))\\frac{x^{2} - 4y}{x - 2\\sqrt{y}} &= lim_(x → (2, 1))((x^{2} - 4y)(x + 2\\sqrt{y}))/((x - 2\\sqrt{y})(x + 2\\sqrt{y})) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → (2, 1))((x^{2} - 4y)(x + 2\\sqrt{y}))/(x^{2} - (2\\sqrt{y})^{2}) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → (2, 1))((x^{2} - 4y)(x + 2\\sqrt{y}))/(x^{2} - 4y) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → (2, 1))(x + 2\\sqrt{y})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → (2, 1))(x + 2\\sqrt{y}) &= 2 + 2\\sqrt{1} \\\\\n&= 2 + 2 \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $4$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124854 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29610715-333d-5e0c-9328-eec1d45f56ed" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits Along Different Paths for Multivariable Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9dd74ec8-86d5-598a-8e56-95b29a0e16e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122147} {:answer/id #uuid "f535756a-536a-5965-94cf-1bc4d691cc78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122148} {:answer/id #uuid "f620f6c6-7569-5934-965b-6637b2ffe002" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122149} {:answer/id #uuid "1e230c9e-1d92-596b-b732-a4e0e88354aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122150} {:answer/id #uuid "36d9ac2d-26fe-5ab7-9c7c-395d60353d4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122151}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f535756a-536a-5965-94cf-1bc4d691cc78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122148} :answer-field/id #uuid "072a5c78-7c03-5585-83aa-d0df143fe86d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122146}] :question/id #uuid "6a81953f-1927-53ea-b174-321fab218b53" :question/math-academy-id "q-35953" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n$lim_(x → (0, 0))\\frac{xy - xy^{2}}{x^{2} + 2xy}$\n\n1. The limit evaluates to $1$ along the path $y = x$\n2. The limit evaluates to $1$ along the path $y =-x$\n3. The limit exists and is equal to $1$"}]}
